{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>Take no interest from him.</b> The fact that it says 'take no interest from him' implies that from a rich man it is permitted to take. And so in parashat Mishpatim (Exodus 22:24) it says: 'If you lend money to My people, to the poor who is with you, etc. — you shall not lay interest upon him.' Therefore the Torah wrote in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 23:21): 'You may lay interest upon a foreigner, but upon your brother you shall not lay interest' — whoever is your brother, you shall not lay interest upon him, even though the borrower profits from this. And go after the reason, for the essential reason for the prohibition of interest is that it removes the trait of trust (bitachon) from a person, for every merchant's eyes are lifted toward HaShem, because he is uncertain whether he will profit or not; but the one who gives at interest — his profit is known and fixed, and he leans on his pledge that is in his hand and removes his heart from HaShem. And the reason the borrower too transgresses a negative commandment is because he causes the lender to sin and removes him from the trait of trust — as is well known from the matter of those who lend at interest, that most of them are of little faith and stout-hearted, far from charity, because their trust is not in HaShem. And the reason it is permitted to give interest to a gentile is that every gentile's presumptive status is that he is mute and a robber: even if he is subjugated under your hand, in any case his way is to come by indirection, and even if his pledge is in your hand you are never secure — neither in the principal nor in the profit; and therefore his eyes are always lifted toward HaShem to save him from his hand. And this is the reason for the prohibition of interest in every case. And the reason it mentioned 'the poor' is that the verse speaks of the common case, for an ordinary poor person is compelled to borrow. And furthermore, you might have thought that if the borrower is poor he does not transgress, for the compulsion brought him to this — it teaches us that even the poor borrower transgresses 'you shall not lay.' And therefore this commandment is juxtaposed to the parashah of the sabbatical year (shemittah), for the reason for shemittah too is that we should place our trust in HaShem." "And it mentions the terms 'neshekh' and 'tarbit,' for on the borrower's side it is called 'neshekh' (a bite), because interest resembles a snake, in the manner of one that bites the heel: it gives a small scratch and goes and swells and inflates up to the crown of the head, and at first it is not noticeable until its inflammation rises beyond healing. And on the lender's side it is called 'tarbit' (increase), for he increases his wealth and property, according to his reckoning — but in truth, in the end his assets disintegrate. And it appears that for this reason the expression 'his hand slackens' (וּמָטָה יָדוֹ) is not mentioned in the entire portion but only in this passage, for it comes to warn the lender lest he collapse — in the manner that this one's hand slackens — lest you too become like him in disintegration: for precisely one whose money he did not give at 'neshekh' will not collapse; but if you give, then you too will become like him, for your assets disintegrate and do not increase, if you think to multiply your wealth through neshekh and tarbit. And for this reason it says 'your money you shall not give him at neshekh, and at tarbit you shall not give your food' — it did not mention the word 'to him' ('lo') by tarbit as it mentioned by neshekh, because the neshekh alone belongs to the borrower, but the tarbit does not belong to the borrower but to the lender. And therefore by tarbit it mentioned 'your food' (אָכְלֶךָ) but not by neshekh, because the lender intends to multiply his wealth and his food, for the expression 'your food' applies only to the lender who intends to eat from the interest; and the expression 'neshekh' applies more to money, for it is not the way of the world to give a se'ah of wheat on condition that he add a kav every week, but all at once he fixes to give five se'ah for four — and if so, he immediately feels his loss, and it is not like a snake that bites a small scratch; but with money the practice is to add one perutah every week, and it is something he does not feel. And it says 'and your brother shall live with you,' for you too shall live, for it says in Ezekiel (Ezekiel 18:13): 'he has given at neshekh, etc. — he shall surely not live,' for he who diminishes this one's livelihood, it is as though he killed him; therefore he too shall surely not live. But one who helps to keep him alive — it is just that he too shall live; therefore it says 'and your brother shall live with you.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:44.847047958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}